-- title:
--   Coefficientwise reduction of p(j(q),j(q^N))
-- statement:
--   Fix an integer $N \ge 1$, a field $L$, a subring $A \subseteq L$, a field $k$ and a ring homomorphism $\mathrm{red} \colon A \to k$, and let $p$ be a polynomial in two variables with coefficients in $A$ (an element of `MvPolynomial (Fin 2) A`). Write $\mathrm{jqModC}\,K = q^{-1}\cdot(E_4^3 \eta^{-24})$ for the Laurent series over a field $K$ obtained from the integral power series `jNum` by coefficient reduction along $\mathbb{Z} \to K$, and $\mathrm{jqNModC}\,K\,N$ for its image under `qExpand`, the exponent-scaling endomorphism $q \mapsto q^N$ of $K((q))$. Then `modularEval N A p` is the evaluation of $p$ in $L((q))$ sending the coefficients into $L((q))$ as constant series and the two variables to $\mathrm{jqModC}\,L$ and $\mathrm{jqNModC}\,L\,N$. Assume this element lies in `modularRing N A`, the subring of $L((q))$ generated by the constant series attached to elements of $A$ together with these two series. The assertion is that applying `redRes`, the coefficientwise application of $\mathrm{red}$ to series all of whose coefficients lie in $A$ (available on `modularRing N A` through its inclusion into `integralCoeffs A`), to this element yields the evaluation of $p$ in $k((q))$ with coefficients reduced by $\mathrm{red}$ and regarded as constant series, and the two variables sent to $\mathrm{jqModC}\,k$ and $\mathrm{jqNModC}\,k\,N$.
--
--   This identity computes the coefficientwise reduction map on the modular ring in terms of the presentation of that ring by evaluation of two-variable polynomials at the $q$-expansions of $j(q)$ and $j(q^N)$: reduction of coefficients of $p$ commutes with evaluation. It is used in the node-localisation analysis of the modular curve, where a statement about the reduction of a $q$-expansion must be converted into one about the reduced polynomial evaluated at the characteristic-$p$ expansions; it is cited in the passage from membership in the modular localisation to membership in the localisation at a point for integral elements.
